# ===========================================================================
# CECODES - Huella de Carbono
#
# Local development:  copy to `.env.local`
# Docker deployment:  copy to `.env`      (docker-compose.yml reads `.env`)
#
# Every variable below already has a working default (see docker-compose.yml), so this file
# exists only to override them. The one exception is a non-container deployment (running the
# built app directly with `node server.js`, no compose): there, DATABASE_URL is the one thing you
# must set, because there is no db service to default it to.
#
# Which is why almost everything below is commented out, and why that matters more than it looks.
# `docker compose` reads this file for ${...} substitution, so an uncommented line REPLACES
# docker-compose.yml's default rather than adding to it. `cp .env.example .env` on its own has to
# leave every default standing, so the handful of lines left uncommented are the ones that cannot
# move a default: they restate it (POSTGRES_*) or belong to a script compose never runs (DEMO_*).
# Uncomment what you actually mean to change.
#
# A note on the quotes below, learned the hard way. `docker compose` strips surrounding quotes
# from an env file, so the values here work as written. `docker run --env-file` does NOT: it
# passes "postgresql://..." through with the quote characters still in the string, and the
# connection then fails with nothing in the message to suggest why. If you ever run a container
# by hand rather than through compose, drop the quotes.
# ===========================================================================

# ===========================================================================
# REQUIRED by the app - and already supplied by docker compose, so still commented out here
#
# src/instrumentation.ts validates DATABASE_URL at boot; nothing else in the runtime schema is
# mandatory. A missing DATABASE_URL fails immediately naming the variable, rather than a 500 on
# the first request. Under docker compose it already defaults to the db service below, so this is
# the one thing worth setting by hand for a deployment that is NOT running that container, e.g.
# a plain `node server.js` pointed at a database you manage yourself.
#
# It is worth knowing exactly how short that list is, because refusing to boot is the most
# expensive thing this file can ask for. As src/lib/env.ts stands today, runtimeSchema holds one
# rule: DATABASE_URL must be there. Nothing else in this file can stop the process. Every other
# variable, mail and SITE_URL included, is checked at boot, named in the log if it is wrong, and
# left running.
#
# That line is drawn where it is because a boot refusal costs far more on some targets than
# others. In a container it is a service that fails to come up, which is what you want. On a
# platform that serves a whole deployment from one process (Vercel), the same throw is every
# route answering 500 at once, health checks included, on a deployment that had just gone out
# green, with the reason visible only in the platform's runtime log. That is not a theory; it
# happened here, and the mail rules were what caused it. They no longer live in that schema. See
# the mail section below, and section 7 of docs/DOCKER_DEPLOYMENT.md.
# ===========================================================================

# --- Database ---
#
# Two URLs for one database. The app runs many short-lived queries over DATABASE_URL; Prisma
# migrations need a session, not a pooled connection, so they use DIRECT_URL. Point either at a
# pooler (pgbouncer, Supavisor) or at plain Postgres directly; nothing here assumes one provider.
#
# Under docker compose, both default to the db service and need no override. Leaving them
# commented out is what makes that default apply, and it is the whole point: an uncommented value
# here replaces `@db:5432` with whatever it names. A copied-and-forgotten `@localhost:5432` names
# the init container itself, where nothing is listening, so init retries 30 times, exits 1, and
# the app never starts (docker-compose.yml gates it on service_completed_successfully) while the
# bundled db sits healthy and unused one hostname away.
#
# Uncomment them only to point this deployment at a Postgres that this stack is NOT running:
#   an external database     "postgresql://user:password@db.example.org:5432/cecodes"
#   a plain `node server.js` "postgresql://cecodes:cecodes-local-dev@localhost:5432/cecodes"
# The second is the non-container case from the header: no db service to default to, so the app
# refuses to start until DATABASE_URL is set. DIRECT_URL follows DATABASE_URL unless a pooler
# fronts it, in which case point DIRECT_URL at the same database without the pooler.
# DATABASE_URL="postgresql://cecodes:cecodes-local-dev@db:5432/cecodes"
# DIRECT_URL="postgresql://cecodes:cecodes-local-dev@db:5432/cecodes"

# Where the db container is published ON THE HOST. Containers never use this; they reach the
# database as db:5432. It exists so host-side tooling (bun run dev, vitest, playwright, every
# bun run db:* script) has a route in, and it is 55432 rather than 5432 so it cannot collide with
# a Postgres already installed on the machine. Change it if 55432 is taken, and keep the
# 127.0.0.1: prefix - the bare form "55432" binds 0.0.0.0 and publishes the database.
# A failed port binding stops the WHOLE stack, not just the db service.
# DB_HOST_BINDING="127.0.0.1:55432"

# init imports the full emission-factor library on a FIRST deployment, when the library still
# holds nothing but the seed's starter subset. Set this to true to load it yourself instead.
# SKIP_FACTOR_IMPORT="true"

# --- The database container itself (used only when db is the one running) ---
#
# docker-compose.yml builds DATABASE_URL's default from these three, so changing one here changes
# the other automatically; there is no separate place to keep them in step.
POSTGRES_USER="cecodes"
POSTGRES_PASSWORD="cecodes-local-dev"
POSTGRES_DB="cecodes"


# ===========================================================================
# THE FIRST ADMIN - scripts/init-db.ts validates these before touching the database
# ===========================================================================

# --- The first admin, created idempotently by prisma/seed.ts ---
#
# This is the ONLY way into a fresh system: self-serve registration is off
# (src/lib/feature-flags.ts, FEATURE_SELF_ONBOARDING = false), so this one account administers
# every tenant.
#
# BOTH are commented out, and that is the safe path rather than an omission. With neither set,
# docker-compose.yml supplies ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@cecodes.local and prisma/seed.ts generates a
# random 24-character password, prints it ONCE in the init container's log the first time the
# admin row is created, and never touches it again:
#
#   docker compose up -d && docker compose logs init     # the password is in that output
#
# The alternative is what this file used to ship: a placeholder long enough to satisfy the
# 12-character rule in src/lib/env.ts, published in this repository, on the account that
# administers everything. `cp .env.example .env && docker compose up -d` created it for anyone
# who read past the aside telling them to change it, and because ADMIN_EMAIL was set too, the
# seed re-asserted that password on every restart.
#
# Uncomment ADMIN_EMAIL to use your own address instead of the default. Do it before the first
# run: the seed finds the admin BY email, so changing it later creates a second admin rather than
# renaming the first. It is required outside compose (`bun run db:seed` has no default to fall
# back on) and init fails naming it.
#
# Uncomment ADMIN_PASSWORD only to choose the password yourself instead of reading the generated
# one, or to recover an account nobody can get into: a value here is re-applied on every later
# run, which keeps the stored password in step with this file and signs the admin out of any open
# session when it actually changes. Minimum 12 characters. There is deliberately no default
# anywhere, because a shipped default password is a vulnerability, not a convenience.
# ADMIN_EMAIL="admin@example.com"
# ADMIN_PASSWORD="<strong-password-min-12-chars>"


# ===========================================================================
# OPTIONAL - sensible defaults, set only if you need them
# ===========================================================================

# Public hostname of this deployment. Two things read it, and only the first is obvious:
#
#   the optional TLS proxy   docker compose --profile edge up -d
#                            Caddy needs it to get a certificate, and DNS must already point at
#                            this server.
#   emailed links            docker-compose.yml builds SITE_URL from it, so password-reset and
#                            welcome mail point at https://<this hostname>.
#
# The second is why it is worth setting even before you enable TLS, and why it used to be worth
# nothing: the app pinned SITE_URL to http://localhost:3000 unconditionally, and SITE_URL wins
# over DOMAIN, so a deployment with working HTTPS still mailed unreachable localhost links and
# nothing anywhere reported an error.
#
# A bare hostname, no scheme, exactly as the Caddyfile expects it. Unset means
# http://localhost:3000, the loopback address this stack publishes: right for a local trial, and
# wrong the moment anyone reaches the app from another machine.
# DOMAIN="huella.example.org"

# Port the app listens on inside its container. Rarely worth changing; the
# published binding lives in docker-compose.yml.
# PORT="3000"

# Public origin used to build the links in password-reset and confirmation
# emails. It is pinned in configuration because the alternative is reading the
# request's Host header, which an attacker controls: a crafted request would
# have this app mail a real user a real token pointing at another site.
#
# Usually you do not need this. DOMAIN above already answers the question, and
# SITE_URL is only for a deployment whose public address differs from it: behind
# a path-based proxy, or on a second hostname. With neither set, docker compose
# falls back to http://localhost:3000, which is reachable from the host running
# the stack and nowhere else. Outside compose, links are built in development
# from the request host and in production not at all, so a reset mail fails
# visibly instead of pointing somewhere unintended.
#
# It must be a full URL including the scheme, not a bare hostname like DOMAIN
# above. The app checks that at boot and NAMES it in the log if it is anything
# else, which is the only warning you get: a value it cannot parse is discarded,
# so the override you set on purpose is ignored and the links fall back to
# DOMAIN, or to nowhere when DOMAIN is unset too. Nothing about it stops the app,
# and it does not turn mail off either, because a link built from DOMAIN still
# reaches the user. Read the boot log after setting it, and check that the reset
# mail points where you meant.
# SITE_URL="https://huella.example.org"

# --- Mail ---
#
# MAIL_TRANSPORT chooses how password-reset mail is sent:
#
#   smtp    Talk to an SMTP server directly. The default, and what docker-compose.yml points at
#           Mailpit for, so a reset link works out of the box with no provider account. Read the
#           messages it caught at http://127.0.0.1:8025.
#   resend  Use the Resend API instead. Set RESEND_API_KEY and MAIL_FROM below when you do.
#   none    No mail at all. Also what an unset or unrecognised MAIL_TRANSPORT is read as.
#
# NOTHING IN THIS SECTION CAN STOP THE APP. That is a change, and it is worth reading twice,
# because it used to work the other way and the other way is what most people expect.
# Every rule below is checked at boot by validateMailConfig() in src/lib/env.ts, which REPORTS
# rather than throws: a problem prints a banner naming the offending variable, and the app carries
# on serving every page it has. These rules used to be fatal, and one wrapped RESEND_API_KEY took
# the entire site down with it, which is the outage the REQUIRED section above describes.
#
# The consequence is worth stating plainly, because the new failure is HARDER to notice than the
# old one, not easier. A mail slip no longer produces a dead site that somebody reports within
# minutes. It produces a site that works perfectly and a password reset that quietly does nothing:
# mailConfigured() answers false, so the reset action refuses up front, writes no token row, and
# tells the user exactly what it tells everyone else. Nobody sees an error. The person locked out
# of their account just never receives the mail.
#
# So the boot log is the only place this surfaces, and the moment to read it is the deploy, not
# the support ticket:
#
#   docker compose logs app | head -40    # compose: the banner is near the top, before Next's own output
#   the deployment's runtime log          # Vercel and anything like it, same banner, same place
#
# SMTP_USER and SMTP_PASSWORD are a pair. Setting one without the other does not refuse the boot;
# it names the missing half in that banner and turns mail off, with the silent consequence above.
# Leaving BOTH unset is the normal case and is what Mailpit wants: it accepts anything and asks
# for nothing. The pairing check exists because one without the other used to send no AUTH at
# all, silently, so a real relay answered "530 Authentication required" on every message with
# nothing anywhere pointing at the half-set variable.
#
# MAIL_TRANSPORT="smtp"
# SMTP_HOST="mailpit"
# SMTP_PORT="1025"
# SMTP_USER=""
# SMTP_PASSWORD=""

# Password-reset email, sent through Resend (https://resend.com), for a production deployment
# that does not want to run its own SMTP relay. Set MAIL_TRANSPORT="resend" to select this
# instead of SMTP above.
#
# Once MAIL_TRANSPORT="resend" is set, both of these are required: a key with no From address
# cannot address a message, and a From address with no key cannot send one. One without the other
# does not stop the app. It is named in the boot banner and mail is off until you fix it.
#
# MAIL_FROM is a From header, so either a bare address or "Name <address>".
# The domain has to be one you verified with Resend.
#
# The key must also be plain visible ASCII, and it is rejected if it is not. That is not pedantry:
# the key is sent as an HTTP header, and a character that cannot go in a header (a line break from
# a wrapped paste, a smart quote, a non-breaking space) is invisible in a .env file. Without the
# check it boots cleanly and then drops every message with one warning, while the app goes on
# telling users to check their inbox. So the rule runs in both places that matter: at boot, where
# an operator is still watching and the banner names the variable, and inside mailConfigured(), so
# the reset is refused up front instead of accepting a request it cannot deliver. What it no
# longer does is take the site down. A key that cannot send mail now costs you mail.
#
# The same check refuses the "<resend-api-key>" placeholder on the line below, because
# uncommenting both lines and then pasting only one of them is a routine slip and an invisible
# one: without it, mail would look configured and every message would be rejected by Resend.
# RESEND_API_KEY="<resend-api-key>"
# MAIL_FROM="CECODES <no-reply@huella.example.org>"


# ===========================================================================
# DEVELOPMENT ONLY - must never be set on a deployed server
# ===========================================================================

# Demo companies and sample activity data (prisma/seed-demo.ts, run with
# `bun run db:seed:demo`).
#
# There is ONE shared production database, so this variable is the only thing
# standing between that script and real client data. scripts/init-db.ts
# refuses to run at all when it is "true", so an automated deployment cannot
# create demo tenants by accident.
DEMO_SEED_ALLOWED="false"
DEMO_PASSWORD="<demo-password>"

# Skips admin creation during `bun run db:seed`. Only for working against a
# database whose admin already exists. Never set this in a deployment: it
# turns the fail-loud check above back into a silent skip.
# SEED_SKIP_ADMIN="false"
